==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|LL.}} {{term|sphincter|lang=la}}, from {{etyl|grc|en}} {{term|σφιγκτήρ||band, contractile muscle|tr=sphinktēr|lang=grc|sc=polytonic}}, from {{term|σφίγγω||I bind tight|tr=sphingō|lang=grc|sc=polytonic}}. Compare [[sphinx]], "the strangler".

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/ˈsfɪŋktə/}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{anatomy}} A [[ringlike]] [[band]] of [[muscle]] that surrounds a bodily opening, [[constrict]]ing and [[relax]]ing as required for normal [[physiological]] functioning.
#* '''1982''', {{w|Lawrence Durrell}}, ''Constance'', Faber & Faber 2004 (''Avignon Quintet''), p. 836:
#*: She decided that she would force him to climax first by the sheer strength of her young animal control, the strength of her '''sphincters''' [...].

